<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-proline biosynthesis I (from L-glutamate)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PROSYN-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; This universal L-proline biosynthesis pathway is found in organisms ranging from bacteria to |FRAME: TAX-9606|, although the input to the pathway, |FRAME:GLT|, is formed by different means in different organisms. For example, in mammals, L-glutamate is formed in the mitochondrial matrix by an NAD(P)&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;-dependent glutamate dehydrogenase (EC 1.4.1.3) and transported to the cytoplasm, where the rest of the reactions occur, while in bacteria glutamate is formed by a NADP+-dependent enzyme (EC 1.4.1.4). The first two enzymes, |FRAME: EC-2.7.2.11| and |FRAME: EC-1.2.1.41|, catalyze the formation of |FRAME: L-GLUTAMATE_GAMMA-SEMIALDEHYDE|, which rearranges spontaneously to form |FRAME: L-DELTA1-PYRROLINE_5-CARBOXYLATE|. In higher plants these two reactions are catalyzed by a single bi-functional enzyme in a single reaction |CITS: [1384052]|. |FRAME: L-DELTA1-PYRROLINE_5-CARBOXYLATE| is converted to |FRAME:PRO| in a single step, catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-1.5.1.2|. In addition to being a major constituent of proteins, proline also acts as an osmotic protectant in bacteria, plants and animals that are under osmotic stress. Abscisic acid (ABA) and salt stress stimulate proline biosynthesis in plants |CITS: [12602867]|. In Arabidopsis, proline can account for up to 20% of the free amino acid pool after salt stress |CITS: [8022935]|. In young Arabidopsis plants, both the ornithine (|FRAME: PWY-3341|) and the glutamate pathways contribute to the increase of proline level during osmotic stress, whereas in adult plants only the glutamate pathway plays an important role in proline accumulation |CITS: [9576796]| . The glutamate pathway was also shown to be the major contributor in proline accumulation upon osmotic stress in salt-stressed month bean plants |CITS: [8103048]|. In Medicago, on the other hand, both the ornithine and the glutamate pathways contribute to the osmotic stress-induced proline accumulation |CITS: [15032841]|.</Description>
